  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full paper text, including appendices, is present; no apparent truncation or document mismatch.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Reports downstream benchmark accuracy across MoUE width and depth settings, bearing on V6 with a different measure from held-out pretraining loss. It does not test the power-law loss or compute-optimal allocation claims.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites scaling laws as motivation but does not fit or test power laws for held-out loss. Figure 5 compares validation loss over training against FLOPs, but reports no fitted law or numeric values; it is an architecture comparison, not a test of V1 or V2. Recorded downstream aggregate accuracy results bear on V6. Individual benchmark scores are omitted in favor of the reported aggregate Avg.",
    "test_reason": "An architecture paper. It cites Kaplan and Hoffmann only as motivation, then compares its MoUE method with matched MoE baselines on downstream accuracy (Tables 1–2, Fig. 6) and on validation loss versus FLOPs (Fig. 5). It fits no power law and makes no extrapolation or allocation analysis. It does not ask whether downstream performance tracks scale or loss predictably. The 'scaling' is extra virtual width or depth from the method itself, so every result compares the study's own method against others. That bears on no version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Broadly right. The authors conclude that MoUE beats matched MoE baselines in width and depth expansion and in warm-start conversion. They claim gains from larger universal pools are 'monotonic' in Table 2, yet Fig. 10 shows degradation beyond 12 UEs per layer, and the JetMoE numbers dip from 10A2 to 12A2.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study changes MoE architecture through cross-layer expert reuse and reports downstream benchmark accuracy; it does not reproduce the original loss-scaling measurements or fitting procedure.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported downstream accuracy varies with expansion setting and pool size; the paper reports degradation when the warm-start universal-expert pool grows beyond its stated optimum of 12 UEs.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "MoUE average accuracy rises as 'virtual parameters' grow at fixed activated and physical parameters (Table 1, Fig. 6). The gain is non-monotonic in warm-start pool size: JetMoE 10A2 50.7 vs 12A2 50.5, and Fig. 10 shows a decline beyond 12 UEs.",
        "proposed_claim": "Effective capacity from cross-layer expert reuse (virtual width) acts as an additional MoE scaling dimension.",
